-`--sort` and `--direction` select the ordering column and direction; both default
-to a timestamp descending sort. Results are unpaginated.
+Options:
+
+- `--actor` (required): actor to match exactly.
+- `--action`: action to match exactly. Omit it to match every action for the actor.
+- `--since`: only rows whose timestamp is at or after this ISO 8601 date (or datetime),
+  e.g. `--since 2026-01-31`.
+- `--sort`: any column of the audit table (`id`, `actor`, `action`, `target`, `ts`);
+  default `ts`.
+- `--direction`: `asc` or `desc`; default `desc`.
+- `--limit`: maximum rows returned, 1 to 1000; default 50.
+- `--offset`: rows to skip, for paging; default 0.
+
+Ordering ties are broken by `id`, so consecutive pages do not overlap.
+
+```sh
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m auditlog.search --actor alice --since 2026-01-01 --limit 20 --offset 20
+```
+
+All values are bound as query parameters; sort columns and directions are checked
+against allowlists because identifiers cannot be bound.
 
 Clause building lives in `auditlog.filters`, so the same escaping and ordering
 rules apply to every query against the table.
